drills.explanation:
Decide the throwable first, then walk the pattern one `{}` at a time:

```java
if (values.length > countPlaceholders(pattern) && values[values.length - 1] instanceof Throwable t) {
    throwable = t;
    values = Arrays.copyOf(values, values.length - 1);
}
while (i < pattern.length()) {
    int j = pattern.indexOf("{}", i);
    if (j < 0 || next >= values.length) break;
    // one backslash before {}: literal {}; two: one backslash, then the argument
}
out.append(pattern.substring(i));
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong formatters. `String.format`-style thinking renders the exception into the text (`order 42 failed` gets the exception's `toString` appended or substituted, and `throwable` is null). Stopping with an error when arguments run out instead of copying the rest fails the "3 of {}" case. Treating every backslash-brace as an escape gets `C:\\{}` wrong: two backslashes mean a literal backslash followed by a real placeholder. `String.valueOf` on an `int[]` prints `[I@1b6d3586` instead of `[1, 2]` (the rendering test).

