drills.explanation:
Put and remove around the chain; capture outside the runnable, install and restore inside it:

```java
static Runnable wrap(Runnable task) {
    Map<String, String> captured = Mdc.getCopyOfContextMap();     // on the caller's thread
    return () -> {
        Map<String, String> previous = Mdc.getCopyOfContextMap(); // on the worker
        if (captured == null) Mdc.clear(); else Mdc.setContextMap(captured);
        try { task.run(); } finally { if (previous == null) Mdc.clear(); else Mdc.setContextMap(previous); }
    };
}
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong versions. Removing `requestId` after the chain returns, instead of in `finally`, leaves it on the thread when the chain throws (the cleanup test). Copying any header value into the MDC lets `abc\nlevel=ERROR` through (the forged-id test). Reading the context inside the runnable, on the worker, captures the worker's empty context (the pool test sees `null`) and also the value at run time rather than at wrap time (the capture-time test). Calling `Mdc.clear()` after the task instead of restoring `previous` wipes the worker's own `worker=w1` entry (the boundary test).

